== Foreword
<foreword>
Mercury occupies a position in classical Indian metallurgy that has no
parallel in Western alchemical traditions. The entire field is named
after it: #emph[rasashastra] — the science of #emph[rasa], where
#emph[rasa] means mercury (the substance that "moves" or "flows,"
cognate with Latin #emph[currere] via the same Indo-European root). The
substance is identified theologically as #emph[Shiva-bija] (Shiva’s
seed) and conceptually as the #emph[bija] (seed) of all medicinal
preparations. Whatever mercury touches, mercury enters; whatever enters
mercury, mercury can transform. This reactive promiscuity, which makes
mercury both technologically useful and biologically dangerous, is
encoded in the tradition as both #emph[amrita] (nectar) and #emph[visha]
(poison) — a duality that the #emph[samskara] sequence is designed to
navigate.

The eighteen classical #emph[samskaras] of mercury
(#emph[ashtadasha-samskara]) constitute one of the most elaborate
purification-and-transformation sequences in any premodern technological
tradition. The first eight are practiced in contemporary Indian pharmacy
under the #emph[deha-vada] (medicinal) branch and reproducibly produce
specific compounds whose modern characterization has been published in
peer-reviewed materials science literature. The remaining ten constitute
the #emph[loha-vada] (alchemical) branch, mostly not currently
practiced, whose claims about element-level metal transmutation are not
supported by modern chemistry but whose described intermediate
substances often correspond to real compounds.

This volume documents both branches with equal seriousness. Where the
chemistry is reproducible, we describe what it produces. Where the
claims are implausible, we say so. Where the tradition’s prescriptions
have computational structure derivable from the broader jyotish
framework, we show the derivation. The goal is neither to validate every
classical claim nor to dismiss the tradition because some claims fail;
it is to produce a faithful, technically accurate reference that serves
practitioners, scholars, and researchers working at the intersection of
these domains.

