== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Mercury is the chemical element with symbol Hg and atomic number 80, the
only metal liquid at standard temperature and pressure. Its density
(13.534 g/cm³ at 20°C) makes a small volume notably heavy in the hand;
its surface tension (~485 mN/m, far higher than water) causes it to form
spherical droplets that resist wetting most surfaces. These two
properties — heaviness and rolling sphericity — figure prominently in
the texts’ descriptions and serve as the basis for several traditional
purity tests.

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
In Sanskrit, mercury appears under multiple names whose etymologies
illuminate different aspects of the substance:

- #strong[Parada] (पारद) — "that which carries across" (root
  #emph[par-], "to bring across"), referring both to mercury’s role as a
  carrier in metallurgical reactions and to its symbolic role in
  carrying the practitioner across saṃsāra
- #strong[Rasa] (रस) — the most common term in technical contexts;
  literally "flowing essence" or "juice," cognate with the Indo-European
  root that also produces Latin #emph[ros] (dew) and Greek #emph[rheos]
  (current/flow)
- #strong[Sūta] (सूत) — "that which is born/begotten"; in the theology,
  #emph[sūta] of Shiva
- #strong[Sūtarāja] (सूतराज) — "king among the sūtas"; recognizes
  mercury’s primacy among medicinal substances
- #strong[Hara-bija] (हर-बीज) — "seed of Hara (Shiva)"
- #strong[Caladhātu] (चलधातु) — "moving metal"
- #strong[Pārada] in the Rasarnava is etymologized as "pārān dadāti" —
  "that which gives liberation"

The terminological diversity reflects the substance’s conceptual
centrality. No other metal in the Indian tradition carries this many
ritually and philosophically loaded synonyms.

=== 1.3 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
In the classical Indian astrological framework, mercury (parada)
corresponds to the #emph[graha] #strong[Budha] (Mercury planet). This
correspondence is internally consistent across primary sources: -
#emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3 - #emph[Brihat
Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy - #emph[Rasaratna
Samuccaya] (Vagbhata, ~13th c.) graha-rasa identifications

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification of grahas: - #strong[Budha
is jeeva-graha] — the "living/cognitive" class - This places mercury in
the same metaphysical category as the sun (Surya, gold) and Jupiter
(Guru, gold/yellow metals) - Notably distinct from the structural/dense
dhatu-grahas (Chandra-silver, Mangala-copper, Shani-iron) and the
dissolutive moola-grahas (Shukra, Rahu, Ketu)

The jeeva classification is significant for the framework: it predicts
that mercury operations should be most favorable when the practitioner’s
mental/cognitive state is alert and integrated, since Budha rules
intelligence and discriminative perception. This connects to traditional
prescriptions that #emph[parada-samskara] be performed by an initiated,
focused practitioner during intervals of mental clarity, not during
illness or distraction.

=== 1.4 Origins (paradotpatti)
<origins-paradotpatti>
The Rasaprakasha-Sudhakara and other texts describe mercury as
originating "#emph[near Himalaya — Girindra mountain]," consistent with
mercury sources in the Himalayan region. Historically, Indian mercury
came primarily from cinnabar (#emph[hingula], HgS) deposits; the
tradition recognizes this, classifying #emph[hingula] as the principal
source mineral and establishing protocols for extracting mercury from
cinnabar through controlled heating with reducing agents.

Modern source areas: Spain (Almadén — historically the largest mine),
Italy (Monte Amiata), western United States, Mexico, China, and Slovenia
(Idrija). The medieval Indian tradition references #emph[Darada Desha]
(northwest Kashmir region) and #emph[Mleccha Desha] (the term for
foreign lands, often China) as principal external sources.

