=== 2.3 Samskaras 1-8 (Deha-vada): chemistry analysis
<samskaras-1-8-deha-vada-chemistry-analysis>
==== 2.3.1 Svedana (fomentation)
<svedana-fomentation>
#strong[Procedure:] Mercury is placed inside a #emph[pottali] (cloth
bundle) along with herbal ingredients (typically including mustard,
#emph[saindhava] lavana, #emph[trikatu], radish, #emph[chitraka],
ginger; proportions: each 1/16th by weight of mercury). The pottali is
suspended in a #emph[dolayantra] containing acidic liquid (kanji) or
specific plant juices and steamed continuously for 3 days (one
#emph[prahara] per day, or 3 hours daily).

#strong[Purpose stated in classical texts:] "#emph[malashaithilya]" —
destabilization of impurities (mala) to make them removable in
subsequent steps.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Mercury surface develops a thin oxide/sulfide
layer through atmospheric exposure (#emph[sapta-kanchuka]). Prolonged
contact with mildly acidic herbal solutions at warm temperatures
(~80-90°C) causes hydrolysis of these surface compounds and partial
dissolution of trace metal impurities. Sulfur-containing herbs (mustard,
asafoetida) provide thiol groups that bind mercury surface contaminants.
Net effect: chemical surface preparation, removal of oxide layer.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Multiple modern studies have
characterized mercury before and after svedana using XRF and AAS (atomic
absorption spectroscopy), confirming reduction of trace metal impurities
(Pb, Sn, Bi) by ~30-50% per pass.

==== 2.3.2 Mardana (trituration)
<mardana-trituration>
#strong[Procedure:] Mercury is triturated in #emph[khalva-yantra]
(mortar and pestle) with various ingredients in sequence — typically
#emph[rasona] (garlic), #emph[saindhava] (rock salt), #emph[triphala]
powder, #emph[aradraka] (ginger). Trituration is continued for 3 days,
~12 hours per day.

#strong[Purpose:] "#emph[chapalatva-haranam]" — removal of mercury’s
"fickleness" (chapalatva), said to result in mercury that no longer
"rolls" or escapes containment as readily.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Mechanical agitation in the presence of
organosulfur compounds (allicin from garlic, glucosinolates from
mustard) and salts produces: - Surface cleaning through abrasion against
sodium chloride crystals - Formation of thin organomercury layer at
surface from sulfur-containing organics - Possible reduction in surface
tension through organic adsorption

The "removal of chapalatva" is plausibly describing reduced mercury
fluidity due to surface-bound organic films, which would also reduce
volatilization losses (a known concern with elemental mercury at room
temperature — vapor pressure ~0.0014 mmHg at 25°C, sufficient to produce
dangerous airborne concentrations).

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Practiced routinely in licensed
rasashastra pharmacy.

==== 2.3.3 Murchana ("swooning" — loss of physical form)
<murchana-swooning-loss-of-physical-form>
#strong[Procedure:] Mercury is triturated with multiple ingredients
including mineral additions until it loses its characteristic shine and
metallic mobility. Ingredients vary by tradition; commonly include
sulfur (gandhaka), mica (abhraka), and various herbs.

#strong[Stated outcome:] Mercury becomes "#emph[nirvirya]" — temporarily
inert; loses #emph[chala] (motion).

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Formation of organomercury and inorganic
mercury compounds. With sulfur present, partial conversion to
mercury(II) sulfide (HgS, both alpha-cinnabar red and beta-metacinnabar
black, depending on temperature). With organic acids, formation of
mercury salts. The "swooning" describes the visible transformation from
liquid metal to solid powder — the shine disappears because metallic
mercury surface has been replaced by oxide/sulfide/organic layers.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. The end-state of murchana, when sulfur
is the principal additive, is essentially #emph[kajjali] — black HgS,
the precursor to many #emph[kupipakwa] preparations.

==== 2.3.4 Utthapana ("revival/resurrection")
<utthapana-revivalresurrection>
#strong[Procedure:] The #emph[murchita] mercury is re-extracted as
elemental mercury through controlled heating, often in a
#emph[patana-yantra] (sublimation apparatus).

#strong[Stated outcome:] Mercury "returns" to its metallic form, but
transformed — said to be "purified at a deeper level."

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Thermal decomposition of mercury compounds
(HgS at ~580°C decomposes to Hg + S, mercury organic salts decompose at
lower temperatures). Mercury vapor distills, condenses to liquid
mercury. The process effectively performs a separation: mercury that has
been chemically combined with impurities through previous steps is
liberated, while non-volatile residues remain behind.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High, but requires proper ventilation due to
mercury vapor toxicity.

==== 2.3.5 Patana (sublimation/condensation)
<patana-sublimationcondensation>
#strong[Procedure:] Three sub-types — #emph[urdhwa-patana] (upward
sublimation), #emph[adho-patana] (downward sublimation),
#emph[tiryak-patana] (lateral sublimation). Mercury is repeatedly
sublimed and condensed in different geometric configurations, each said
to remove specific dosha types.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Distillation purification. Mercury’s boiling
point (357°C) makes vapor-phase purification straightforward. Different
geometric arrangements (urdhwa \= vapor rises, adho \= vapor descends
through cool zone, tiryak \= vapor traverses laterally) achieve
different separation efficiencies for impurities of different vapor
pressures and condensation behaviors.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Modern distillation produces mercury of
\>99.99% purity.

==== 2.3.6 Rodhana ("awakening")
<rodhana-awakening>
#strong[Procedure:] The purified mercury is rested in a sealed vessel
containing salt water (typically #emph[saindhava] solution) for 3 days.
Said to "restore mercury’s potency" after the depleting effects of
previous purifications.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Resting in salt solution allows surface
re-equilibration, possibly removal of trace dissolved impurities through
ion exchange with the salt solution. The "restoration of potency"
framing may refer to recovery of surface reactivity after distillation
has produced an extremely clean (and therefore atypically passive)
surface.

#strong[Reproducibility:] Practiced; effects on mercury composition
difficult to characterize precisely with modern instruments because the
changes are subtle.

==== 2.3.7 Niyamana (controlling)
<niyamana-controlling>
#strong[Procedure:] Trituration with specific herbal pastes (typically
including #emph[rasona], #emph[bhringaraja], #emph[amlika],
#emph[navasagara], #emph[musta]) for 3 days. Said to remove
"#emph[chapalatva]" (fickleness/instability) more thoroughly than
earlier steps.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Repeated mardana-like processing with
organosulfur and organonitrogen compounds. #emph[Navasagara] (ammonium
chloride) is significant: NH₄Cl reacts with mercury at elevated
temperatures to produce mercury(II) ammine complexes, which then
decompose on further heating. This appears to be a controlled
introduction of nitrogen-coordinated mercury species.

#strong[Reproducibility:] Practiced.

==== 2.3.8 Dipana / Sandipana ("stimulation/appetizing")
<dipana-sandipana-stimulationappetizing>
#strong[Procedure:] Final samskara of the deha-vada series. Mercury is
steamed in dolayantra with kanji or #emph[aranala] for one prahara (3
hours).

#strong[Stated outcome:] Mercury becomes "#emph[grahana-yogya]" —
capable of swallowing, digesting, or amalgamating other substances. This
is the #emph[prerequisite] for subsequent loha-vada operations and for
incorporation into kupipakwa preparations.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Final acidic conditioning of mercury surface.
Acidic reactivation removes any residual oxide/passivation layer that
might prevent subsequent chemical reactions. The "appetite" framing is
technically apt — the surface is rendered chemically active for amalgam
and compound formation.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High; this completes the deha-vada sequence
and the resulting product is what most published makaradhwaja and
swarna-bhasma studies use as starting material.

=== 2.4 Samskaras 9-18 (Loha-vada): assessment
<samskaras-9-18-loha-vada-assessment>
These ten samskaras are largely not practiced in mainstream contemporary
rasashastra. The textual descriptions are detailed but the procedures,
taken as a sequence aimed at gold-from-base-metal transmutation, are not
chemically possible.

However, the procedures often describe specific intermediate operations
whose chemistry IS real. The Atlas recovery framework’s contribution is
to identify which intermediates correspond to real compounds and which
claims are aspirational.

==== 2.4.1 Gaganagrasa, Charana (mica-swallowing, addition of grasa)
<gaganagrasa-charana-mica-swallowing-addition-of-grasa>
Mercury triturated with successive additions of mica (#emph[abhraka])
until the mercury "swallows" significant mica weight. Modern
interpretation: mercury does not actually dissolve mica (silicate), but
mechanical mixing produces a fine mercury-mica composite. Described
"weight conservation" claims are not validated.

==== 2.4.2 Garbhadruti, Bahyadruti (inner/outer liquefaction)
<garbhadruti-bahyadruti-innerouter-liquefaction>
Procedures for liquefying solid metal additions either inside or outside
mercury. Internal (#emph[garbha]) liquefaction described as occurring
within the mercury "womb"; external (#emph[bahya]) liquefaction
occurring around it.

Modern interpretation: amalgamation of soft metals (gold, silver, tin,
lead) IS real. Mercury at room temperature dissolves gold up to ~16 wt%,
silver to ~30%, tin to ~42%, lead to ~3.5%. The "inner/outer"
distinction may describe primary amalgamation (metal dissolves into
mercury) vs. eutectic surface alloy (metal-mercury phase forms at
interface without full dissolution).

==== 2.4.3 Jarana (digestion)
<jarana-digestion>
Detailed in lohavada\_reconstruction.py. Mercury "digests" gold, mica,
sulfur in claimed weight-conservation. #emph[Real chemistry:]
amalgamation. #emph[Unverified claim:] weight conservation across the
operation.

==== 2.4.4 Ranjana (coloring)
<ranjana-coloring>
The mercury preparation acquires specific colors (most often deep red)
through processing with #emph[rakta-tailam] (red oil) containing wax,
honey, blood, #emph[takana] (borax), earthworm extract, and lead bhasma.
Modern interpretation: formation of α-HgS (cinnabar) nano-dispersions in
organic matrix, structurally similar to vermilion/sindoor. The red color
is real and reproducible. The subsequent claim that this preparation
transmutes base metals is not.

==== 2.4.5-2.4.7 Sarana, Kramana (mobilizing, penetrating)
<sarana-kramana-mobilizing-penetrating>
Procedures said to enhance mercury’s ability to permeate other metals.
Modern interpretation: amalgam-forming behavior is well-characterized;
mercury does diffuse into lattice of soft metals. The textual claims
about "deep penetration" beyond surface-mediated amalgamation are not
supported.

==== 2.4.8 Vedha (transmutation)
<vedha-transmutation>
#strong[The terminal claim of the loha-vada sequence:] processed mercury
added to base metal causes transmutation to gold. #strong[Modern
chemistry:] not possible at chemical (non-nuclear) energies.
#strong[Real process producing visually similar results:]
mercury-gilding (fire-gilding), in which a Hg-Au amalgam is applied to
base metal substrate, then heated to drive off mercury, leaving a thin
gold layer.

This is documented historical metallurgy. Indian, European, East Asian,
and Middle Eastern smiths used mercury-gilding through the 19th century.
The procedure produces gold-appearing surfaces on copper, silver, or
iron substrates. Whether classical rasashastra practitioners considered
this distinct from element-level transmutation is a question for textual
scholarship, not chemistry.

==== 2.4.9 Bhakshana (internal consumption)
<bhakshana-internal-consumption>
Final samskara: the fully processed mercury is consumed by the
practitioner, with claims of immortality (#emph[amaratva]) and bodily
transformation. #strong[Modern medicine: this is dangerous and
contraindicated.] Even processed mercury preparations carry risk of
mercury poisoning with chronic use. Modern Ayurvedic practice limits
even properly processed mercurial preparations to small doses (typically
\<250 mg) for short durations under qualified supervision, with clinical
monitoring of urinary mercury excretion.

