== 3. The Dosha Framework
<the-dosha-framework>
=== 3.1 Naisargika doshas (natural impurities)
<naisargika-doshas-natural-impurities>
The three primary doshas inherent to raw mercury per #emph[Rasaratna
Samuccaya]:

- #strong[Visha (विष)] — poison; the inherent toxicity of mercury
- #strong[Vahni (वह्नि)] — fire; the heat-related instability
- #strong[Mala (मल)] — impurity; physical contaminants

Modern chemistry maps these to: - Visha → mercury’s inherent toxicity
(mercury is a cumulative poison, all forms) - Vahni → mercury’s
volatility (vapor pressure → airborne mercury → poisoning) - Mala →
trace metal contaminants from source ore

=== 3.2 Yogika doshas (combinational)
<yogika-doshas-combinational>
Two named secondary doshas: - #strong[Naga (नाग)] — lead contamination -
#strong[Vanga (वङ्ग)] — tin contamination

Both are real concerns: cinnabar deposits often contain lead and tin
minerals that contaminate extracted mercury. Modern AAS analysis of
crude mercury from various sources confirms 10s to 100s of ppm Pb and Sn
typical, requiring removal for medical use.

=== 3.3 Sapta kanchuka doshas (the seven coverings)
<sapta-kanchuka-doshas-the-seven-coverings>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] 11.19:

#block(inset: (left: 1em), stroke: (left: 2pt + luma(160)))[
#emph[parpaṭī pāṭanī bhēdī drāvī malakarī tathā |] \
#emph[andhakārī tathā dhvāṁkṣī vijñēyāḥ sapta kaṁcukāḥ ||]
]

The seven outer coverings: 1. #strong[Parpati] — produces scales on skin
2. #strong[Patani] — causes ruptures 3. #strong[Bhedi] —
tearing/ulceration 4. #strong[Dravi] — liquefying (causes tissue
dissolution) 5. #strong[Malakari] — generates impurities 6.
#strong[Andhakari] — causes blindness 7. #strong[Dhvanksi] — causes
darkness of skin

Modern interpretation per Dharmawiki and standard texts: "Kanchuka layer
is formed by the oxidation of impure metals present in the impure
mercury. On contact with oxygen in the air, these metallic impurities
form a film on the surface of mercury exposed to air."

The clinical effects listed (skin scaling, ulceration, blindness, etc.)
are #emph[consistent with mercury poisoning symptoms] — chronic mercury
toxicity does cause skin lesions, vision impairment, and tissue damage.
The classical texts are describing real toxicological effects with
traditional terminology.

=== 3.4 Five gatis (mercury loss modes)
<five-gatis-mercury-loss-modes>
Practical concerns during processing — the ways mercury escapes:

+ #strong[Jala-gati] — loss in water (during washing)
+ #strong[Mala-gati] — loss with impurities (during sublimation
  residues)
+ #strong[Hamsa-gati] — "swan-like" floating-away movement
+ #strong[Dhuma-gati] — vapor loss (mercury volatilization)
+ #strong[Jiva-gati] — invisible/unattributable loss

The first four describe known real loss mechanisms during mercury
processing. The fifth is acknowledged in the texts as "not preventable
except by Ishwara’s grace," which can be read as a candid admission that
mercury yield is never 100% and the "missing" portion cannot always be
accounted for.

The five gatis are operationally significant: a competent #emph[vaidya]
tracks mercury through processing precisely because losses are
inevitable and controlling them is part of the craft. This is documented
industrial-process awareness in a tradition often dismissed as merely
mystical.

