== 4. Major Mercurial Preparations
<major-mercurial-preparations>
=== 4.1 Hingula (cinnabar) — the source mineral
<hingula-cinnabar-the-source-mineral>
#strong[Composition:] α-HgS (cinnabar), space group P3₁21, hexagonal
crystal structure \
#strong[Color:] brilliant red (the eponymous "cinnabar red") \
#strong[Hardness:] 2.0-2.5 Mohs \
#strong[Density:] 8.176 g/cm³

Hingula is the principal naturally-occurring source of mercury and is
itself used in Ayurvedic preparations after #emph[shodhana]. The
classical texts classify hingula as a #emph[sadharana rasa] per
Vagbhata’s classification system.

#strong[Modern characterization:] XRD studies of pharmaceutical-grade
hingula show pure α-HgS with trace impurities (Fe, Cu, sometimes Sb) at
levels consistent with natural cinnabar provenance.

=== 4.2 Kajjali (black mercury sulfide compound)
<kajjali-black-mercury-sulfide-compound>
#strong[Composition:] Hg + S triturated together until fine black
powder, principally β-HgS (metacinnabar) with some unreacted reagents \
#strong[Color:] intense black with metallic glint when freshly prepared
\
#strong[Use:] intermediate compound for many subsequent preparations;
precursor to makaradhwaja

#strong[Preparation:] Equal parts purified mercury and purified gandhaka
triturated together for an extended period (traditionally 24+ hours)
until the mercury is completely consumed and the mixture is uniform
black.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] Mechanochemical synthesis of mercury sulfide.
Pure HgS is the target compound; in practice, kajjali is a mixture of
α-HgS, β-HgS, residual elemental sulfur, and some unreacted mercury. The
pharmaceutical preparation typically tests for #emph[nishchandratva] (no
metallic shine when finely powdered) which is the empirical proxy for
completion of Hg+S reaction.

=== 4.3 Sindoor / Rasa-Sindoora
<sindoor-rasa-sindoora>
#strong[Composition:] purified α-HgS (alpha-cinnabar phase) \
#strong[Color:] bright red/vermilion \
#strong[Use:] therapeutic, ritual (married Hindu women’s #emph[kumkum]),
iconographic (Hanuman murtis are traditionally orange-red)

#strong[Preparation:] Kajjali is heated in a #emph[kupipakwa]
(bottle-method) apparatus. The black β-HgS converts to red α-HgS through
thermal phase transition (β → α at ~344°C with appropriate heating
profile). The product sublimates and condenses on the inside neck of the
bottle as a bright red sublimate.

#strong[Modern chemistry:] The β-α phase transition in HgS is
well-characterized. The α form (cinnabar) is thermodynamically stable
below ~344°C; β (metacinnabar) is metastable but kinetically forms first
under most synthesis conditions. The kupipakwa procedure exploits this —
black metacinnabar is heated until it converts to red cinnabar,
condensing as the stable phase.

#strong[Reproducibility:] Routine in licensed rasashastra pharmacy.
Modern XRD shows \>95% α-HgS in well-prepared rasa-sindoora.

=== 4.4 Makaradhwaja
<makaradhwaja>
#strong[Composition:] Au-Hg-S compound; predominant phase HgS with
embedded gold nanoparticles \
#strong[Color:] bright red \
#strong[Classical formula:] Triguna Makaradhwaja (3 parts S : 1 part Hg
+ Au), Shadguna Makaradhwaja (6 parts S : 1 part Hg + Au)

#strong[Preparation:] Purified mercury, purified sulfur, and gold leaf
(24-carat) are processed through extensive #emph[bhavana] (trituration
with herbal juices) followed by kupipakwa in graded heat. Final product
extracted from inside the bottle as bright red sublimate.

#strong[Modern characterization:] - ICP-OES studies (Khedekar et al.):
12,131 ppm gold as principal element along with mercury and sulfur -
XRD: pure HgS hexagonal phase, with small amounts of metallic gold below
detection threshold of bulk XRD - FTIR: indicates presence of organic
compounds from herbal processing - TEM: Au nanoparticles embedded in HgS
matrix (Singh et al. on swarnabhasma show analogous nano-gold
structures)

#strong[Therapeutic claims:] Used for #emph[jwara] (fever),
#emph[prameha] (diabetes), #emph[agnimandya] (poor digestion),
#emph[vajikarana] (vitalization), rheumatoid arthritis. Modern clinical
studies show some efficacy for arthritis but raise concerns about
long-term mercury toxicity.

#strong[Modern medical perspective:] Even when properly prepared,
makaradhwaja contains mercury that can be released bioavailably.
Multiple case reports document mercury poisoning from inappropriate use.
Licensed Ayurvedic practice limits dose to 30-125 mg twice daily for
short durations under medical supervision; mass-market or
self-prescribed use is dangerous.

=== 4.5 Rasa-Pushpa, Rasa-Karpura
<rasa-pushpa-rasa-karpura>
Variant kupipakwa preparations producing different mercury compounds: -
#strong[Rasa-pushpa] ("mercury flower"): white sublimate, primarily
HgCl₂ (mercuric chloride) when prepared with chloride-containing salts -
#strong[Rasa-karpura] ("mercury camphor"): white crystalline, similar
HgCl₂ with different processing yields purer crystalline form

Both are highly toxic in their own right (mercuric chloride is strongly
poisonous) but used historically in small doses for specific conditions.

=== 4.6 Other named preparations
<other-named-preparations>
The #emph[Rasendra Sara Sangraha] and #emph[Rasa Tarangini] describe
additional mercurial preparations including #emph[Hinguleshwara Rasa],
#emph[Anandabhairava Rasa], #emph[Tribhuvankirti Rasa],
#emph[Kasturibhairava Rasa], etc. Each has specific composition and
indication. Modern characterization is incomplete for most; published
analytical work focuses on the most common preparations (makaradhwaja,
swarnabhasma, sindoor).

