== 6. Modern Toxicological Context
<modern-toxicological-context>
=== 6.1 Mercury toxicity is real
<mercury-toxicity-is-real>
Mercury in any form is toxic to humans. The forms differ in toxicity
profile: - #strong[Elemental mercury (Hg⁰):] highly volatile; vapor
inhalation causes neurotoxicity (tremor, cognitive impairment), kidney
damage. Skin absorption minor. Oral ingestion of liquid mercury mostly
passes through GI tract without major absorption (a fact that has saved
some accidental ingestions). - #strong[Inorganic mercury salts (Hg²⁺):]
highly toxic orally and dermally; HgCl₂ is corrosive and acutely lethal
at gram doses. Cumulative; affects kidneys primarily. - #strong[Organic
mercury (methylmercury, dimethylmercury):] most dangerous form; crosses
blood-brain barrier and placenta; causes severe neurotoxicity (Minamata
disease). Dimethylmercury is acutely lethal at very small doses through
skin absorption (Karen Wetterhahn case, 1996). - #strong[Mercury sulfide
(HgS):] least bioavailable form; cinnabar/sindoor consumed as medicine
has very low absorption (\<0.2% per various studies). However, "low" is
not "zero," and chronic use produces accumulation.

=== 6.2 Bhasma forms
<bhasma-forms>
Properly prepared mercury bhasmas (HgS-based) have lower bioavailability
than equivalent doses of mercury salts. This is the basis for the
rasashastra claim that processed mercury is "safe." However: -
Bioavailability is reduced, not eliminated - Improperly prepared bhasmas
can contain elemental Hg or HgCl₂, dramatically increasing toxicity -
Long-term cumulative dose is the issue, not single-dose toxicity -
Quality control varies enormously across producers

=== 6.3 Regulatory status
<regulatory-status>
- #strong[United States:] FDA has issued warnings about Ayurvedic
  medicines containing heavy metals; some products banned.
  Mercury-containing preparations are not legally permitted as
  supplements.
- #strong[European Union:] Strict limits on heavy metal content in
  herbal medicines. Most mercurial bhasmas exceed limits.
- #strong[India:] Licensed Ayurvedic products may contain mercury per
  AYUSH regulations, but standards exist for processing and dose.
  Ministry of AYUSH publishes monographs specifying acceptable
  preparations.
- #strong[Australia, UK:] Generally restricted; case-by-case import for
  individual practitioners.

=== 6.4 Honest assessment
<honest-assessment>
The classical tradition’s claim that processed mercury can be safely
consumed has #emph[partial] validation: properly prepared HgS-based
preparations have lower bioavailability than raw mercury. The claim that
mercurial preparations confer immortality or substantial life-extension
is #emph[not] validated by any modern study. Long-term mercury exposure
is associated with neurodegenerative outcomes, not longevity.

Practitioners using mercurial Ayurvedic preparations should: 1. Use only
properly licensed, quality-controlled products 2. Limit doses to those
specified in classical texts (typically 30-250 mg for makaradhwaja-type
preparations) 3. Limit duration of use (typically \<30 days continuous)
4. Monitor with periodic testing (urinary mercury, kidney function) 5.
Avoid in pregnancy, in children, and in patients with kidney impairment

