== 10. Closing Notes
<closing-notes>
This volume covers mercury (parada) as a complete unit. It is not a
survey or summary; it is a structured reference work intended to serve
practitioners, scholars, and researchers working on rasashastra,
materials chemistry of traditional preparations, and computational
modeling of classical Indian sciences.

The volume is honest about scope. The 18-samskara sequence is documented
at face value; modern chemistry is described where it applies; the gap
between traditional claim and modern interpretation is identified
explicitly. The Atlas framework’s contribution is structural — it can
derive timing predictions for any mercury operation and identify where
intermediate substances correspond to real compounds.

Mercury is the most chemically complex and culturally loaded of the
dhatus. Volume I covers it. Subsequent volumes will treat the other
major dhatus (gold, silver, copper, iron, tin, lead, zinc, brass)
following the same structure, adapted to each metal’s specific chemistry
and tradition.

The Atlas Compendium accumulates over time. New volumes add to the
reference; existing volumes are updated as new research emerges or
framework refinements occur. The compendium is not a finished work; it
is infrastructure for ongoing work.

🙏

